          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state. Her surrounding remarks emphasize that her difficulty is expressing the love verbally, not an absence of love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I am sure, my love’s\\nMore ponderous than my tongue.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The focal avowal is immediately and grammatically qualified by “According to my bond,” so it invokes and affirms Cordelia’s existing filial obligation rather than creating a new promise. Her next turn identifies that bond as duties of obedience, love, and honour. This contextual material directly explains the force of this occurrence; it is not merely a separate nearby promise. The undertaking force is substantial but subordinate to the direct avowal of love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"Although Cordelia calls herself unhappy and speaks under pressure, the focal words are a controlled, measured avowal qualified by the exact extent of her filial bond. They are not represented as a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The focal expression’s core work is adequately represented by T and P: it avows love while affirming and limiting that love according to a filial bond. Its contrast with her sisters also resists Lear’s demand for inflated speech, but the supplied occurrence does not require a separate residual mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most while allocating portions of his kingdom. After refusing ornamental amplification, Cordelia tells Lear that she loves him according to her filial bond and no more or less.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia affirms an existing filial bond whose duties she describes as returning Lear’s care through obedience, love, and honour. She does not make a new future promise or undertake unlimited devotion; she expressly limits the bond, anticipating that marriage would divide her love, care, and duty. Lear does not accept the declaration as an adequate response to his contest: he tells her to amend it lest she damage her fortunes.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The passage is recognised as the opening love-test scene from William Shakespeare’s King Lear. The assessment uses only the supplied text.\",\"limitations\":\"The excerpt provides strong evidence for the represented avowal and filial-bond affirmation, including Cordelia’s aside, but it cannot conclusively establish private sincerity beyond the scene’s representation or the bond’s full practical terms. No omitted context is treated as absent from the work.\"}"
